 {"q": "What is the real difficulty with Luke's census 'when Quirinius was governor of Syria'?", "options": ["Rome never held censuses", "Josephus dates Quirinius's census of Judea to AD 6, ten years after Herod's death, while Luke places the birth under Herod", "Quirinius was a Jewish king, not a Roman governor", "Luke does not mention Herod at all"], "answer": 1, "why": "Luke 1:5 places the birth under Herod; Josephus puts Quirinius's census in AD 6. Scholars still debate how to reconcile them."},
 {"q": "Jesus argues more with the Pharisees than with any other group. What is the best conclusion?", "options": ["He was a Sadducee", "He rejected Judaism altogether", "He was in many ways close to them: you argue most with those who share your questions", "The Pharisees were Roman officials"], "answer": 2, "why": "Like the Pharisees, he applied the law to daily life and believed in resurrection; the arguments are among neighbours."},
 {"q": "Why are the Gospels written in Greek, when Jesus spoke Aramaic?", "options": ["Because Jesus taught only in Greek", "Because Greek was the shared language of the eastern Roman empire, so his words reached the wider world through it", "Because Aramaic had no alphabet", "Because the Romans banned Aramaic"], "answer": 1, "why": "After Alexander, Greek was the common language from Egypt to Syria; the Gospels keep a few of his Aramaic words."},
